+### Numbered Steps
+1.  **Multi-Component Transformation (Component Axis):** Apply a 1D lifting-based DWT along the component axis (depth) for each spatial location $(x, y)$ [S2]. Use the Haar lifting scheme for the Luminance component $Y$, and the 5/3 lifting scheme for Chrominance components $U$ and $V$ [S2]. This decomposes the volume into subbands: Low-Low-Low (LLL), Low-Low-High (LLH), Low-High (LH), and High (H) relative to the component axis [S2].
+2.  **Spatial Decomposition:** Apply a 2D spatial wavelet lifting scheme to each of the generated subband frames [S2]. The 2D DWT is implemented as a tensor product of 1D DWTs along the Horizontal ($H$) and Vertical ($V$) dimensions [S1]. For a given subband, apply 1D-DWT along $H$, then along $V$ (or vice versa) [S1]. This yields four spatial sub-bands per component subband: $L_H L_V, L_H H_V, H_H L_V, H_H H_V$ [S1]. The decomposition can be iterative (Mallat scheme), where only the low-pass sub-band ($L_{LLL}$) is further decomposed, or applied uniformly [S1]. [S2] illustrates examples where high-pass components may be transformed fewer levels or not at all.
+3.  **Coding:** Code the spatially decomposed frames using EBCOT coding (JPEG2000 Part-1 standard) [S2].
 
